<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-adana-kebab-at-home">How to Make Adana Kebab at Home</h2>



<p>Once you have your ingredients prepped and your skewers ready, the rest comes down to technique. It&rsquo;s not difficult, just a few key steps to follow for the best results. Here&rsquo;s how I break it down:</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-preparing-your-kebab-mixture">Preparing your kebab mixture:</h3>



<p><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Chop and Drain the Red Peppers</strong></p>



<p>Start by removing the seeds and chopping the red peppers as finely as you can. This helps them blend seamlessly into the meat mixture, rather than showing up as visible chunks.</p>



<p>After chopping, place the peppers in a fine mesh strainer and press gently to release their juices. This step is essential as we want to remove as much liquid as possible so the mixture doesn&rsquo;t become too wet or soggy when it&rsquo;s time to shape.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe.jpg" alt="Meat mixture in a bowl from the top view." class="wp-image-82227" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Kebab-Adana-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Prepare the Meat Mixture</strong></p>



<p>In a large mixing bowl, combine the drained chopped peppers, ground lamb, paprika, red pepper flakes, salt, and black pepper. Using clean hands, mix everything together until fully combined and evenly seasoned.</p>



<p>Once it&rsquo;s mixed, divide the meat into equal portions based on how many skewers you plan to use. My set came with three skewers, so I divided the mixture into three even parts. You can eyeball it or, if you prefer precision, weigh the portions using a kitchen scale.</p>



<p>If you want to make it ahead, just cover the bowl and place it in the fridge. In fact, if you can plan ahead, I highly recommend it. Giving the mixture a chance to rest helps the flavors come together and settle into the meat. An overnight rest (up to 24 hours) can really make a difference.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-shaping-your-adana-kebabs">Shaping your Adana Kebabs</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob.jpg" alt="The meat is being threaded onto the skewer." class="wp-image-82228" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kabob.jpg"></figure>



<p><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Thread Onto the Skewers</strong></p>



<p>When you&rsquo;re ready to thread the meat, start by setting up your workstation. Grab your skewers, a small bowl of water to wet your hands, and bring out the meat mixture from the fridge. </p>



<p>To thread, take one portion of the ground lamb mixture, shape it into a ball, and gently insert the skewer through the center. Using your hands, press and spread the meat down the length of the skewer, turning it as you go to create an even shape. </p>



<p>If or when the mixture starts to feel sticky, lightly wet your hands to prevent the meat from sticking too much. Just be careful not to overdo it. A little water goes a long way, and too much can make the mixture too soft to hold together. </p>



<p>As you work, do your best to keep the thickness even from top to bottom. Once you&rsquo;ve shaped the kebab, take a moment to gently press and seal the top and bottom ends of the meat onto the skewer.</p>



<p><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Create the signature ridges</strong></p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers.jpg" alt="Adana skewers shaped from the top view." class="wp-image-82229" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebab-skewers.jpg"></figure>



<p>If you&rsquo;ve had Adana Kebab before, you&rsquo;ve probably noticed the pattern of shallow ridges pressed into the surface. They&rsquo;re not just for appearance. These indentations help the meat cook more evenly over high heat and give the kebab its signature look.</p>



<p>To make them, use your thumb and forefinger to gently press along the length of the meat, making shallow marks every couple of inches. Rotate the skewer and repeat the same motion on the other side.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-grilling-your-kebabs">Grilling Your Kebabs</h3>



<p><strong><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Grill</strong></strong></p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe.jpg" alt="Lamb kebabs on the grill." class="wp-image-82234" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Turkish-lamb-kebab-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<p>The traditional way to cook Adana Kebab is over a charcoal grill, which gives it that distinct smoky taste and slightly crisp exterior. That said, I used my gas grill for this version to keep it simple and approachable for home cooks. Both methods work well, and below you&rsquo;ll find tips for each.</p>



<p><strong>If using a gas grill:</strong> Preheat your grill to 400&deg;F to 425&deg;F. Clean the grill grates thoroughly and brush them with oil to prevent sticking. </p>



<p>Carefully place the Adana kebab skewers on the grates and grill, turning them frequently to ensure even cooking and a nice char on all sides. </p>



<p>Depending on the thickness and length of your kebabs, this will take about 10 to 12 minutes.</p>



<p>Because the meat is rich in fat, you may see flare-ups as it cooks. That&rsquo;s completely normal, but it&rsquo;s important to stay close to the grill while they&rsquo;re cooking.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Adjust the heat as needed and continue rotating the skewers to avoid burning and help them cook evenly.</p>



<p>I<strong>f using a charcoal grill: </strong>Light your charcoal and let the coals burn until they&rsquo;re covered with a thin layer of white ash. </p>



<p>Spread them out evenly and position the skewers directly over the heat. Grill the kebabs, turning them every couple of minutes to ensure even cooking and that nice, smoky char. </p>



<p>The total time will be similar, around 10 to 12 minutes, again depending on the heat level and the size of your skewers.</p>



<p>Similar to cooking it on a gas grill, keep a close eye during grilling, especially if you notice flare-ups. The goal is juicy, charred kebabs that stay intact from grill to plate.</p>



<p><strong>Step 6 &ndash; Let It Rest While Making Sumac Onions</strong></p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe.jpg" alt="Sumac onions before and after they are mixed." class="wp-image-82231" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-recipe.jpg"></figure>



<p>Once the kebabs are grilled, transfer them to a sheet pan and cover loosely with foil. Let them rest for about 5 to 10 minutes so the juices have time to settle back into the meat.</p>



<p>While the kebabs are resting, you can prepare one of the classic accompaniments to Adana Kebab: sumac onions, a traditional onion salad that beautifully balances the richness of the meat. </p>



<p>They&rsquo;re easy to make, but if you&rsquo;d like a more detailed breakdown, feel free to check out my <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/sumac-onions/">Sumac Onions recipe</a>.</p>



<p>To make a quick batch, combine sliced red onion, olive oil, lemon juice, kosher salt, sumac, and a pinch of red pepper flakes in a medium bowl. Using clean hands, gently massage the onions until they have softened slightly. Finish with a sprinkle of chopped parsley.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-assembling-and-serving">Assembling and Serving</h3>



<p><strong>Step 7 &ndash; Assemble:</strong></p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs.jpg" alt="Adana wrap being assembled and placed on a plate from the top view." class="wp-image-82232" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs-350x217.jpg 350w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Adana-kebabs.jpg"></figure>



<p>With the meat grilled and your sumac onions ready, all that&rsquo;s left is the bread. Traditionally, Adana Kebab is served with lavash, a soft Mediterranean-style flatbread.</p>



<p>It&rsquo;s similar to <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/homemade-pita-bread-recipe/">pita bread</a>, and you can usually find it in most supermarkets. Of course, if you&rsquo;d like to try making your own, I also have a <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/lavash-bread-recipe/">homemade lavash recipe</a> available for you to follow.</p>



<p>To assemble, place a piece of lavash flat on a plate and use it to slide the meat off the skewer. Since my skewers are quite large, I like to cut the kebab in half while it&rsquo;s still on the skewer, then slide each half off gently. The meat should release easily.</p>



<p>From there, simply top it with a generous spoonful of <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/sumac-onions/">sumac onions</a>, a few slices of tomatoes, and a sprinkle of fresh parsley before rolling it up.</p>



<p>Needless to say, it is best to enjoy your Adana wrap while it&rsquo;s still warm.</p>


